lessons.content:
## Data and variables

JavaScript programs work with data. Beau notes that the language has **seven data types**. The ones you will see first are strings (text) and numbers. A variable is simply a name you use to store a piece of data so you can use it later.

In this part of the course, variables are declared with the `var` keyword. (Later lessons introduce `let` and `const`.)

## Declaring and assigning

You can declare a name first and give it a value later, or do both in one statement. The assignment operator is a single equals sign `=`.

```js
var a;
console.log(a);
// Expected output:
// undefined

var b = 2;
a = 7;
b = a;
console.log(a);
console.log(b);
// Expected output:
// 7
// 7
```

An uninitialized variable holds `undefined` until you assign something. After `b = a`, both names store the same value, `7`.

A common pattern is to declare several names, then initialize them:

```js
var a = 5;
var b = 10;
var c = "I am a";
```

## Case sensitivity and camelCase

Variable names are **case-sensitive**. `myVar` and `myvar` are two different variables. If you assign to one and log the other, you will not see the value you expect.

The usual naming style in JavaScript is camelCase: start with a lowercase letter and capitalize the start of each later word, as in `myVarName` or `camelCase`.

```js
var myVar = 10;
var myvar = 3;
console.log(myVar);
console.log(myvar);
// Expected output:
// 10
// 3
```

## The seven data types

Beau lists seven data types in this lesson. You do not have to memorize a textbook chapter on each one yet, but you should know the list exists:

- `undefined`
- `null`
- `boolean`
- `string`
- `symbol`
- `number`
- `object`

Strings and numbers will show up constantly in the next few lessons. Booleans (`true` / `false`), `undefined`, and objects arrive in more depth later.

## Common pitfalls

- Logging a variable you have declared but not assigned, and being surprised by `undefined`.
- Treating `myVar` and `myvar` as the same name.
- Confusing the assignment operator `=` with a comparison. Comparison operators come later.

## Key takeaways

- JavaScript has seven data types; strings and numbers are the ones you use first.
- `var` declares a variable. `=` assigns a value.
- A declared but uninitialized variable is `undefined`.
- Names are case-sensitive; camelCase is the usual style.

## Arithmetic operators

Almost every program eventually counts, totals, or scales something, so number operators are some of the first tools you need. JavaScript treats ordinary integers and decimals as the same `number` type in this beginner material: you do not switch to a separate “decimal type” just because a value has a point in it.

Numbers are a basic data type in JavaScript. You can add, subtract, multiply, and divide them with `+`, `-`, `*`, and `/`.

```js
var sum = 10 + 10;
var difference = 45 - 33;
var product = 8 * 10;
var quotient = 66 / 33;
console.log(sum, difference, product, quotient);
// Expected output:
// 20 12 80 2
```

Division of two numbers can produce a whole number or a decimal, depending on the values.

## Increment, decrement, and decimals

To add one to a variable, you can write `myVar = myVar + 1`, or the shorter increment operator `myVar++`. To subtract one, use `myVar--`.

```js
var myVar = 87;
myVar++;
console.log(myVar);
// Expected output:
// 88

myVar--;
console.log(myVar);
// Expected output:
// 87
```

JavaScript also has decimal (floating-point) numbers. You write them with a decimal point, and you can use the same arithmetic operators:

```js
var ourDecimal = 5.7;
var product = 2.0 * 2.5;
var quotient = 4.4 / 2.0;
console.log(product, quotient);
// Expected output:
// 5 2.2
```

## The remainder operator

Remainder is easy to underestimate because it looks like a minor cousin of division, but it shows up in real programs: wrapping around a list, deciding even versus odd, or checking whether a count divides evenly into a group size.

The remainder (modulo) operator `%` gives the leftover after division. `17 % 2` is `1`, because 2 goes into 17 eight times with 1 left over. This is a standard way to test whether a number is even or odd: even numbers have remainder `0` when divided by `2`.

```js
console.log(17 % 2);
console.log(48 % 2);
// Expected output:
// 1
// 0
```

## Compound assignment

Because `myVar = myVar + 5` is so common, JavaScript has compound operators that update a variable in place:

| Operator | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `+=` | Add and assign |
| `-=` | Subtract and assign |
| `*=` | Multiply and assign |
| `/=` | Divide and assign |

```js
var a = 3;
a += 12; // 15
a -= 6;  // 9
a *= 2;  // 18
a /= 3;  // 6
console.log(a);
// Expected output:
// 6
```

## Common pitfalls

- Mixing up `%` (remainder) with `/` (division).
- Forgetting that `++` and `--` change the variable itself, not just the value in an expression you forgot to store.
- Expecting decimal arithmetic to always be “clean”; floating-point math can be slightly imprecise, even though the examples in this lesson use tidy values.

## Key takeaways

- Use `+`, `-`, `*`, and `/` for ordinary arithmetic.
- `++` and `--` add or subtract one from a variable.
- `%` returns the remainder; `n % 2 === 0` is the usual even test.
- `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, and `/=` update a variable in place.

## What a string is

A string is a sequence of characters — letters, digits, spaces, and punctuation — used for text. In JavaScript you write a string literal by wrapping the text in quotes. You can use double quotes or single quotes. Both are valid; pick one style and stay consistent in a file.

```js
var firstName = "Alan";
var lastName = "Turing";
console.log(firstName, lastName);
// Expected output:
// Alan Turing
```

The quotes are not part of the stored text. They only tell JavaScript where the string begins and ends.

## Escaping quotes

Sometimes the text itself needs a quote character. If the string is wrapped in double quotes, a raw `"` inside it would look like the end of the string and cause a syntax error. The fix is to *escape* the inner quote with a backslash:

```js
var sentence = "He said, \"Hello.\"";
console.log(sentence);
// Expected output:
// He said, "Hello."
```

The backslash is an escape character: it tells JavaScript to treat the next character as data, not as syntax. You can also wrap the string in single quotes and then use double quotes inside it without escaping, or the other way around.

```js
var alt = 'He said, "Hello."';
console.log(alt);
// Expected output:
// He said, "Hello."
```

Other useful escape sequences you will meet when formatting text include `\'`, `\"`, and `\\` (a literal backslash). Newline and tab have escapes too (`\n`, `\t`), which are handy when you want a string that spans more than one visual line.

## Common pitfalls

- Starting a string with one kind of quote and ending it with the other.
- Putting an unescaped quote of the same kind inside the string, which cuts the literal short and usually produces a syntax error.
- Forgetting that the backslash is not printed unless you escape it as `\\`.

## Key takeaways

- Strings are text values, written in single or double quotes.
- A backslash escapes the next character so a quote or backslash can appear inside the string.
- You can often avoid escaping by using the other quote style for the outer wrapper.

## In practice

Whenever you build a message for a user, a log line, or a label, you are working with strings. Getting quotes and escapes right is a small skill, but it is the difference between a program that runs and a syntax error that stops you before you have written any logic.

## Concatenation with +

You often need to join pieces of text: a first name and a last name, a label and a number, or several short phrases into one sentence. In this lesson Beau builds strings with the `+` operator. When both sides are strings, `+` concatenates them rather than adding numbers.

```js
var first = "Ada ";
var last = "Lovelace";
var name = first + last;
console.log(name);
// Expected output:
// Ada Lovelace
```

Notice the space. JavaScript will not insert spaces for you. If you write `"Ada" + "Lovelace"` you get `AdaLovelace`. Put the space in one of the pieces, or add `" "` between them.

You can also concatenate a string with a variable that already holds a string:

```js
var mid = "World";
var result = "Hello " + mid + ".";
console.log(result);
// Expected output:
// Hello World.
```

## Appending with +=

If you already have a string in a variable and you want to add more text to the end of it, compound assignment works for strings too:

```js
var sentence = "JavaScript is";
sentence += " powerful.";
console.log(sentence);
// Expected output:
// JavaScript is powerful.
```

`sentence += " powerful."` means “take the current value of `sentence`, join this new piece on the right, and store the result back in `sentence`.” It is the same idea as `+=` for numbers, applied to text.

You can append a variable as well as a literal:

```js
var adjective = "fun";
var line = "Learning is ";
line += adjective;
console.log(line);
// Expected output:
// Learning is fun
```

## Common pitfalls

- Forgetting the space between words when you glue two strings together.
- Using `+` between a number and a string and being surprised by concatenation (for example `"Score: " + 10` becomes `"Score: 10"`). This lesson focuses on string-to-string joins; be aware that `+` changes meaning if one side is text.
- Writing `= +` instead of `+=` when you meant to append.

## Key takeaways

- `+` concatenates strings.
- Spaces and punctuation must be included in the pieces you join.
- `+=` appends to an existing string variable and stores the longer string in the same name.

## In practice

Most beginner programs spend a surprising amount of time assembling text: greetings, status lines, and sentences built from variables. If a result looks 'glued together' or 'too spaced out,' look at the literals you concatenated and fix the spaces there, rather than blaming the operator.

If you later move on to template literals, you will have another way to assemble text. The skill in this lesson still matters: you need to see the pieces of a sentence and know which characters (especially spaces) belong in which piece.

## Length

Every string has a `length` property: the number of characters in it, including spaces and punctuation. You read it with dot notation.

```js
var name = "Beau";
console.log(name.length);
// Expected output:
// 4

var greeting = "Hi there";
console.log(greeting.length);
// Expected output:
// 8
```

`length` is not a function, so you do not write `name.length()`. You just read the property.

## Bracket notation

You can pull out a single character by its position. JavaScript counts from zero: the first character is at index `0`, the second at `1`, and the last at `name.length - 1`.

```js
var firstName = "Ada";
console.log(firstName[0]);
console.log(firstName[2]);
console.log(firstName[firstName.length - 1]);
// Expected output:
// A
// a
// a
```

The last-character pattern, `str[str.length - 1]`, is worth memorizing. It works for a string of any length.

You can also reach other positions from the end, such as the second-to-last character at `str[str.length - 2]`.

## Strings are immutable

Individual characters in a string cannot be reassigned. This does not work:

```js
var myStr = "Jello World";
// myStr[0] = "H"; // does not change the string
```

If you want a new string, build a new value and assign it to the variable, for example by concatenating a new first letter with the rest of the original text:

```js
var myStr = "Jello World";
myStr = "H" + myStr.slice(1);
console.log(myStr);
// Expected output:
// Hello World
```

(`slice` is a convenient way to take the tail of a string. The important idea in this lesson is that you replace the whole string, you do not mutate one character in place.)

## Common pitfalls

- Calling `length` as a function (`length()`).
- Forgetting that the first character is at index `0`, so the last is at `length - 1`, not `length`.
- Trying to write `str[0] = "A"` and wondering why the string did not change.

## Key takeaways

- `str.length` is the number of characters.
- Bracket notation `str[i]` reads the character at index `i`, starting at 0.
- The last character is `str[str.length - 1]`.
- Strings are immutable: change them by assigning a new string, not by editing one index.

## In practice

You will often need the first or last character of a piece of input, or you will need to know how long a password or a name is. Reach for `length` and bracket notation first. If you need to change a letter, build a new string and assign it back.

## A Mad Libs style string

This lesson puts concatenation into a small word game. The idea is the same as Mad Libs: you are given a sentence with blanks, and you fill those blanks with words you choose — a noun, a verb, an adjective, and an adverb — to produce a complete (often silly) sentence.

Beau builds the result by concatenating literal pieces of the sentence with variables that hold the chosen words. The function receives those words as parameters so you can reuse the same sentence template with different fillers.

## Building the sentence

A typical version looks like this:

```js
function wordBlanks(myNoun, myAdjective, myVerb, myAdverb) {
  var result = "The " + myAdjective + " " + myNoun + " " + myVerb + " to the store " + myAdverb + ".";
  return result;
}

console.log(wordBlanks("dog", "big", "ran", "quickly"));
// Expected output:
// The big dog ran to the store quickly.
```

Read that assignment carefully. The literal fragments (`"The "`, `" "`, `" to the store "`, `"."`) supply grammar and spacing. The parameters supply the words. If you leave out a space in a literal, two words will crash into each other: `Thebigdog...`.

You can pass different words and get a different sentence from the same template:

```js
console.log(wordBlanks("cat", "small", "walked", "slowly"));
// Expected output:
// The small cat walked to the store slowly.
```

The point is not the story about a dog or a cat. The point is that a reusable function can assemble a longer string from parts.

## Common pitfalls

- Forgetting spaces around the inserted words.
- Hard-coding the example words inside the function instead of using the parameters, so every call prints the same sentence.
- Returning nothing, or logging inside the function but not returning the string, if the exercise asks you to return the result.

## Key takeaways

- You can build a complete sentence by concatenating literals and variables.
- A function with parameters lets you reuse the same template with different words.
- Spacing has to be part of the literal pieces you join.

## In practice

This little word game is a pattern you will reuse: a function that takes small pieces of data and returns a larger, formatted string. Once you can do that reliably, greetings, reports, and user messages all become easier to write.

## What an array is

An array is an ordered list of values, written in square brackets with commas between the entries. The values can be numbers, strings, booleans, or even other arrays. Arrays let you keep a collection under one name instead of inventing `item1`, `item2`, `item3`, and so on.

```js
var ourArray = ["John", 23];
var yourArray = ["Beau", 30];
console.log(ourArray);
// Expected output:
// [ 'John', 23 ]
```

## Indexes and mutation

Like strings, arrays are indexed from zero. `array[0]` is the first element, `array[1]` is the second, and so on.

Unlike strings, array elements **are** mutable. You can assign to an index and the array changes in place:

```js
var myArray = [18, 64, 99];
myArray[0] = 45;
console.log(myArray);
// Expected output:
// [ 45, 64, 99 ]
```

That is one of the most important contrasts in this part of the course: `myStr[0] = "H"` does not change a string, but `myArray[0] = 45` does change an array.

## Nested arrays

An array can contain other arrays. This is sometimes called a multi-dimensional array. You read a value with two (or more) sets of brackets: the first index chooses the inner array, the second chooses the element inside it.

```js
var nested = [["the universe", 42], ["everything", 101010]];
console.log(nested[0][0]);
console.log(nested[1][1]);
// Expected output:
// the universe
// 101010
```

`nested[0]` is `["the universe", 42]`. `nested[0][0]` is `"the universe"`. You can also assign through that same path if you want to change a nested value.

A nested array is still just data. Later lessons will use the same idea for a shopping list, where each inner array is one item and a quantity.

## Common pitfalls

- Using index `1` when you meant the first element (which is at `0`).
- Assuming arrays are immutable because strings are.
- Reading `nested[0, 1]` as if it were two indexes. In JavaScript you must write `nested[0][1]`.

## Key takeaways

- Arrays are ordered, zero-indexed lists written in `[...]`.
- You can change an element by assigning to its index.
- Arrays can contain arrays; use chained brackets to reach inner values.

## In practice

Anytime you have a collection — a list of scores, names, or settings — an array is the default structure to reach for. Learn to count from zero, to replace an item by index, and to nest a list inside a list when each item is itself a group of values.

## Adding and removing at the end: push and pop

Arrays come with methods — functions that belong to the array — for adding and removing items. `push` appends one or more elements to the **end** of the array and returns the new length. `pop` removes the **last** element and returns the element that was removed.

```js
var ourArray = ["Stimpson", "J", "cat"];
ourArray.push(["happy", "joy"]);
console.log(ourArray);
// Expected output:
// [ 'Stimpson', 'J', 'cat', [ 'happy', 'joy' ] ]

var removed = ourArray.pop();
console.log(removed);
console.log(ourArray);
// Expected output:
// [ 'happy', 'joy' ]
// [ 'Stimpson', 'J', 'cat' ]
```

`push` and `pop` change the original array. You do not have to assign the array back to itself.

## Adding and removing at the front: unshift and shift

`shift` is the front-of-the-array version of `pop`: it removes the **first** element and returns it, and every later element slides one index to the left. `unshift` is the front-of-the-array version of `push`: it inserts an element at the beginning and shifts the rest to the right.

```js
var myArray = [["John", 23], ["cat", 2]];
var shifted = myArray.shift();
console.log(shifted);
console.log(myArray);
// Expected output:
// [ 'John', 23 ]
// [ [ 'cat', 2 ] ]

myArray.unshift(["Paul", 35]);
console.log(myArray);
// Expected output:
// [ [ 'Paul', 35 ], [ 'cat', 2 ] ]
```

Beau walks a similar example with `["Stimpson", "J", "cat"]`: after `shift()` the array is `["J", "cat"]`; after `unshift("Happy")` it is `["Happy", "J", "cat"]`.

## Which method to use

| Method | End or front | Adds or removes | Return value |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `push` | end | adds | new length |
| `pop` | end | removes | removed element |
| `unshift` | front | adds | new length |
| `shift` | front | removes | removed element |

If you only remember one picture, remember this: push and pop work at the end; unshift and shift work at the start.

## Common pitfalls

- Confusing `pop` with `shift` and removing the wrong end of the list.
- Forgetting that these methods mutate the array, so later code sees the changed version.
- Mixing up the return value of `push` (a length) with the return value of `pop` (the removed item).

## Key takeaways

- `push` / `pop` add and remove at the end.
- `unshift` / `shift` add and remove at the beginning.
- All four change the array in place.
- `pop` and `shift` return the element they removed.

## In practice

Think of a queue or a stack of plates. Adding and removing at the end (`push` and `pop`) is one pattern; adding and removing at the front (`unshift` and `shift`) is the other. If you pick the wrong pair, your list will still change, but the item you wanted will not be the one that moved.

## Reusable code

A function is a reusable chunk of code with a name. You define it once, then *call* it whenever you want that work done. Beau’s point in this segment is that functions stop you from copying the same steps over and over.

You declare a function with the `function` keyword, a name, a pair of parentheses, and a body in curly braces:

```js
function reusableFunction() {
  console.log("Hi World");
}

reusableFunction();
// Expected output:
// Hi World
```

The parentheses after the name are required when you call it. Writing `reusableFunction` without `()` refers to the function but does not run it.

## Parameters and arguments

Functions become useful when they accept inputs. The names in the parentheses of the definition are *parameters*. The values you pass in at the call site are *arguments*.

```js
function functionWithArgs(a, b) {
  console.log(a + b);
}

functionWithArgs(1, 2);
functionWithArgs(7, 9);
// Expected output:
// 3
// 16
```

Inside the body, `a` and `b` behave like variables that have already been assigned the values you passed in. Each call can supply different arguments, so the same function can add `1` and `2` one moment and `7` and `9` the next.

You can have zero parameters, as in `reusableFunction`, or several, separated by commas.

This is the same idea you will use for almost every later exercise: put the work in a function, pass the data in, and (in the next lessons) return a result.

## Common pitfalls

- Defining a function but never calling it, then wondering why nothing printed.
- Calling it without parentheses.
- Mixing up the order of arguments when there are two or more parameters.

## Key takeaways

- A function packages reusable steps under a name.
- You define it with `function name() { ... }` and run it with `name()`.
- Parameters are inputs in the definition; arguments are the values you pass in a call.
- The same function can be called many times with different arguments.

## In practice

If you notice yourself copying the same three lines to a new place in the file, that is a signal to write a function. Give it a clear name, pass the values it needs, and call it whenever that job comes up again.

## Global and local scope

Scope is the region of a program where a variable name can be seen. In this lesson Beau contrasts two kinds of scope that you get with `var`.

A *global* variable is declared outside any function. Any function in the file can read and change it. That is convenient, and also dangerous: two functions can accidentally overwrite the same name.

A *local* variable is declared inside a function (including a function parameter). It exists only while that function is running. Code outside the function cannot see it.

```js
var myGlobal = 10;

function fun1() {
  oopsGlobal = 5; // no var: this leaks into the global scope
}

function fun2() {
  var output = "";
  if (typeof myGlobal !== "undefined") {
    output += "myGlobal: " + myGlobal;
  }
  if (typeof oopsGlobal !== "undefined") {
    output += " oopsGlobal: " + oopsGlobal;
  }
  console.log(output);
}
```

If you assign to a name inside a function *without* `var` (or, later, `let` or `const`), you can accidentally create a global. That is one of the classic traps in this lesson.

## Local names hide outer ones

You can use the same name in different functions and they do not clash, because each function has its own local scope. You can also declare a local variable with the same name as a global one. Inside the function, the local name *shadows* the global: the function sees the local value, and the global is left alone.

```js
var outerWear = "T-Shirt";

function myOutfit() {
  var outerWear = "sweater";
  return outerWear;
}

console.log(myOutfit());
console.log(outerWear);
// Expected output:
// sweater
// T-Shirt
```

The function returns `"sweater"`. After it finishes, `outerWear` in the outer scope is still `"T-Shirt"`.

This is a feature, not a bug: it lets a function use a convenient name without trashing a global value. It is also a source of confusion if you thought you were updating the global and you actually declared a new local.

## Common pitfalls

- Forgetting `var` (or `let` / `const`) on an inner assignment and polluting the global scope.
- Declaring a local with the same name as a global and wondering why the global did not change.
- Trying to read a local variable from outside its function.

## In practice

Prefer to keep data local unless several functions truly need to share it. Globals make short demos easier and large programs harder to trust. When you do need a shared value, declare it on purpose, at the top, rather than inventing it with a typo inside a function.

## Key takeaways

- Global variables are visible throughout the file; local variables live inside a function.
- Assigning without a declaration keyword can create an accidental global.
- A local variable can shadow a global of the same name without changing the global.

## Functions can send a value back

So far, some functions have only logged to the console. Logging is useful for you, the programmer, but it does not give the rest of your program a value to use. The `return` keyword does that: it immediately finishes the function and hands a value back to the caller.

```js
function timesFive(num) {
  return num * 5;
}

console.log(timesFive(5));
console.log(timesFive(2));
// Expected output:
// 25
// 10
```

The call `timesFive(5)` is replaced by the returned number `25`. You can store that result in a variable, use it in a larger expression, or pass it to another function.

```js
var result = timesFive(3);
console.log(result + 1);
// Expected output:
// 16
```

## What happens if you do not return?

If a function reaches the end of its body without hitting `return`, the returned value is `undefined`. That is why a function that only uses `console.log` gives `undefined` if you try to assign its call to a variable.

```js
function add(a, b) {
  console.log(a + b); // prints, but does not return
}

var value = add(2, 3);
console.log(value);
// Expected output:
// 5
// undefined
```

The `5` is printed by `console.log` inside the function. `value` is still `undefined` because nothing was returned.

A function can have more than one `return`, but the first one that runs wins: the function stops there and any later lines are skipped. That idea becomes important with conditionals in the next lessons.

## Common pitfalls

- Logging a result and forgetting to `return` it, then seeing `undefined` at the call site.
- Putting more work after a `return` and wondering why it never runs.
- Returning too early, before you have computed the value you wanted.

## In practice

Decide what the function is *for*. If other code needs the answer, return it. Use `console.log` to inspect values while you write, not as a substitute for a return value in finished functions.

## Key takeaways

- `return` sends a value back to the caller and ends the function.
- A call can be stored or used as part of a larger expression.
- A function that does not return anything yields `undefined`.

A good habit is to write the `return` first, as a reminder of what the function owes the caller, then fill in the calculation above it. If you find yourself logging a value and also needing it later, return it and let the caller log if necessary.

## A queue

This lesson uses an array as a *queue*: items enter at the back and leave from the front. Think of people standing in line. The next person to be served is the one who has been waiting the longest, at the front of the line. A new person joins at the end.

In JavaScript, that pair of operations is `push` (add to the end) and `shift` (remove from the front). Beau writes a function that takes a queue and a new item, adds the item to the queue, and returns the item that was at the front.

## The function

```js
function nextInLine(arr, item) {
  arr.push(item);
  return arr.shift();
}

var testArr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
console.log("Before: " + JSON.stringify(testArr));
console.log(nextInLine(testArr, 6));
console.log("After: " + JSON.stringify(testArr));
// Expected output:
// Before: [1,2,3,4,5]
// 1
// After: [2,3,4,5,6]
```

`JSON.stringify` is just a convenient way to print the array clearly in a log. The important behavior is: `6` is pushed onto the end, `1` is shifted off the front and returned, and `testArr` is now `[2, 3, 4, 5, 6]`.

Notice that the function mutates the array it was given. Arrays are passed by reference in the sense that the function receives the same array object, not a copy. After the call, the caller sees the changed queue. That is what you want for a real line of people: there is one line, not a copy of the line.

## Common pitfalls

- Using `pop` instead of `shift`, which would remove the person who just arrived instead of the person at the front.
- Returning the new item instead of the item that left the queue.
- Forgetting that the original array is changed, and later code still expects the old contents.

## In practice

Queues show up in support tickets, print jobs, and event handling: work waits in order and is processed in order. Once you can implement “add at the back, remove from the front,” you have a pattern you can reuse for any first-in, first-out list.

## Key takeaways

- A queue adds at the end and removes from the front.
- `push` plus `shift` implements that pattern on an array.
- The function in this lesson returns the removed front item.
- The original array is modified; there is a single shared queue.

## Making decisions with if

Programs need to choose a path. An `if` statement runs a block only when a condition is true. The condition sits in parentheses, and the body sits in curly braces.

```js
function test(value) {
  if (value) {
    return "It was true";
  }
  return "It was false";
}

console.log(test(true));
console.log(test(false));
// Expected output:
// It was true
// It was false
```

JavaScript will also treat some non-boolean values as true or false in a condition. Values that count as false (often called falsy) include `false`, `0`, `""` (the empty string), `null`, `undefined`, and `NaN`. Most other values are truthy, including the string `"false"` and any non-zero number.

## Comparison operators

You usually do not pass a raw boolean. You build a condition with a comparison:

| Operator | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `==` | Equal, after type conversion |
| `===` | Strict equal, no type conversion |
| `!=` | Not equal, after type conversion |
| `!==` | Strict not equal |
| `>` `<` `>=` `<=` | Greater or less than (or equal) |

`==` and `===` are easy to confuse. `==` will coerce types, so `1 == "1"` is true and `3 == "3"` is true. `===` compares value and type, so `1 === "1"` is false. Beau recommends getting used to the strict operators so you are not surprised by hidden conversions.

```js
console.log(1 == "1");
console.log(1 === "1");
console.log(3 === 3);
// Expected output:
// true
// false
// true
```

You can also compare strings. Comparisons are case-sensitive: `"A"` and `"a"` are not the same.

## else and else if

`else` runs when the `if` condition is false. `else if` chains further tests, in order, until one of them succeeds.

```js
function testSize(num) {
  if (num < 5) {
    return "Tiny";
  } else if (num < 10) {
    return "Small";
  } else if (num < 15) {
    return "Medium";
  } else if (num < 20) {
    return "Large";
  } else {
    return "Huge";
  }
}

console.log(testSize(0));
console.log(testSize(23));
// Expected output:
// Tiny
// Huge
```

Order matters. If you tested `num < 20` first, a `0` would already be labeled `"Large"` and would never reach the `"Tiny"` branch.

## Logical operators

You can combine conditions:

- `&&` (and) is true only if both sides are true.
- `||` (or) is true if at least one side is true.
- `!` (not) flips a boolean: `!true` is `false`.

```js
function testLogical(val) {
  if (val > 10 && val < 20) {
    return "Yes";
  }
  return "No";
}

console.log(testLogical(15));
console.log(testLogical(25));
// Expected output:
// Yes
// No
```

`val > 10 && val < 20` describes a range. With `||` you can accept either of two situations, for example a value that is less than 10 *or* greater than 20.

## Common pitfalls

- Using a single `=` in a condition, which assigns a value instead of comparing.
- Using `==` when you meant `===` and getting a match you did not intend (`"1" == 1`).
- Writing overlapping `else if` tests in the wrong order.
- Forgetting that `0` and `""` are falsy, so `if (count)` will skip a perfectly valid zero.

## In practice

Almost every useful program branches: validate input, pick a label, decide whether to continue. Write the condition first in plain language (“if the number is under five…”), then translate it into operators. Prefer `===` and `!==` unless you have a specific reason to allow type conversion.

## Key takeaways

- `if`, `else if`, and `else` choose which block runs.
- Falsy values include `false`, `0`, `""`, `null`, `undefined`, and `NaN`.
- Prefer strict comparison (`===`, `!==`) over loose comparison (`==`, `!=`).
- `&&`, `||`, and `!` combine or flip conditions.
- The order of `else if` tests matters.

## A scoring table as conditionals

This lesson turns a chain of `if` / `else if` tests into a small golf scoring program. In golf, the names of scores describe how a player's strokes compare to par (the expected number of strokes for the hole).

Beau encodes that table in a function `golfScore(par, strokes)` that returns a string name:

| Strokes compared to par | Name |
| --- | --- |
| 1 stroke | Hole-in-one! |
| par - 2 | Eagle |
| par - 1 | Birdie |
| equal to par | Par |
| par + 1 | Bogey |
| par + 2 | Double Bogey |
| par + 3 or more | Go Home! |

The exact names and the hole-in-one special case are the point of the exercise: you have to test the conditions in an order that does not hide an earlier, more specific case.

## Implementing the table

A straightforward translation is a chain of tests. A hole-in-one is simply `strokes === 1`, regardless of par. After that, you compare `strokes` to `par`:

```js
function golfScore(par, strokes) {
  if (strokes === 1) {
    return "Hole-in-one!";
  } else if (strokes <= par - 2) {
    return "Eagle";
  } else if (strokes === par - 1) {
    return "Birdie";
  } else if (strokes === par) {
    return "Par";
  } else if (strokes === par + 1) {
    return "Bogey";
  } else if (strokes === par + 2) {
    return "Double Bogey";
  } else {
    return "Go Home!";
  }
}

console.log(golfScore(4, 1));
console.log(golfScore(4, 2));
console.log(golfScore(5, 8));
// Expected output:
// Hole-in-one!
// Eagle
// Go Home!
```

Using `<= par - 2` for eagle covers both “two under” and even better (except the hole-in-one, which already returned). The final `else` catches everything else, which in this table is three or more over par.

This is the same `else if` skill as the previous lesson, applied to a real naming table. If the order were wrong — for example, if you tested `strokes === par` first in a way that shadowed a more specific case — you would get the wrong nickname.

## Common pitfalls

- Forgetting the hole-in-one special case, so a one-stroke hole is labeled as an eagle or better only by accident.
- Testing “greater than par” too early and swallowing bogey, double bogey, and “go home” as one bucket when the table wants three names.
- Mixing up `par - 1` (birdie) and `par + 1` (bogey).

## In practice

Whenever you have a table of named ranges, a carefully ordered `if` / `else if` chain is a clear way to encode it. Write the table down first, then translate each row into a condition, most specific cases first.

## Key takeaways

- Golf score names depend on strokes compared with par, plus a special case for one stroke.
- An `else if` chain can implement a lookup table.
- Test more specific cases before more general ones.

## Choosing among many exact values

When you want to pick a path based on a value that must match exactly — a number, a string, or another discrete case — a `switch` statement can be clearer than a long `else if` chain.

A `switch` compares an expression to one `case` after another. When it finds a match, it runs that case’s code. You almost always end a case with `break`, which exits the switch. If you leave `break` out, execution *falls through* into the next case, which is sometimes useful and often a bug.

```js
function caseInSwitch(val) {
  var answer = "";
  switch (val) {
    case 1:
      answer = "alpha";
      break;
    case 2:
      answer = "beta";
      break;
    case 3:
      answer = "gamma";
      break;
    case 4:
      answer = "delta";
      break;
  }
  return answer;
}

console.log(caseInSwitch(1));
console.log(caseInSwitch(3));
// Expected output:
// alpha
// gamma
```

Matching uses strict comparison, like `===`. `case 1` will not match the string `"1"`.

## default and grouping cases

A `default` clause runs when no case matches, the way `else` does for `if`:

```js
function switchOfStuff(val) {
  var answer = "";
  switch (val) {
    case "a":
      answer = "apple";
      break;
    case "b":
      answer = "bird";
      break;
    default:
      answer = "stuff";
  }
  return answer;
}

console.log(switchOfStuff("a"));
console.log(switchOfStuff("z"));
// Expected output:
// apple
// stuff
```

You can also group cases that should share a result by stacking them and letting the first ones fall through to the code in the last of the group:

```js
function sequentialSizes(val) {
  var answer = "";
  switch (val) {
    case 1:
    case 2:
    case 3:
      answer = "Low";
      break;
    case 4:
    case 5:
    case 6:
      answer = "Mid";
      break;
    case 7:
    case 8:
    case 9:
      answer = "High";
      break;
  }
  return answer;
}

console.log(sequentialSizes(2));
console.log(sequentialSizes(8));
// Expected output:
// Low
// High
```

Here fall-through is deliberate: values 1, 2, and 3 all produce `"Low"`.

A switch can return a value directly from each case if you prefer that style, but the pattern above — assign, `break`, then return once — is the one Beau works through first.

## Common pitfalls

- Forgetting `break` and accidentally running several cases.
- Expecting loose equality; `switch` uses strict matching.
- Leaving out `default` and getting an empty or leftover result when the input is unexpected.
- Grouping cases but putting a `break` too early, so later values in the group never reach the shared result.

## In practice

Use `if` when you have ranges or complex conditions (`val > 10 && val < 20`). Use `switch` when you have a list of exact values that each need a response, or a few groups of exact values that share a response.

## Key takeaways

- `switch` matches an expression to exact `case` values with strict equality.
- End a case with `break` unless you want fall-through on purpose.
- `default` handles unmatched values.
- Stacked cases can share one block of code.

## Returning a boolean directly

A function that only answers yes or no often hides a comparison behind an `if`. This lesson shows a shorter, clearer pattern: return the comparison itself.

These two functions do the same work:

```js
function isLessVerbose(a, b) {
  if (a < b) {
    return true;
  } else {
    return false;
  }
}

function isLess(a, b) {
  return a < b;
}

console.log(isLess(10, 15));
console.log(isLess(15, 10));
// Expected output:
// true
// false
```

`a < b` is already a boolean. Wrapping it in `if` just to return `true` or `false` is extra machinery. Returning the condition is easier to read and harder to get backwards.

## Early return

A related idea is the early return: as soon as you know the answer, return it, and skip the rest of the function. You will use this in later card-counting and lookup exercises, and it already appeared with `return` stopping a function immediately.

```js
function abTest(a, b) {
  if (a < 0 || b < 0) {
    return undefined;
  }
  return Math.round(Math.pow(Math.sqrt(a) + Math.sqrt(b), 2));
}
```

If either input is negative, the function returns `undefined` right away and never runs the calculation. That is cleaner than nesting the real work inside a large `else`.

The lesson’s main habit, though, is the simple one: if your `if` only exists to return `true` or `false`, replace it with `return someCondition`.

## Common pitfalls

- Writing `return a < b ? true : false` or a full `if` when `return a < b` is enough.
- Returning a string like `"true"` instead of the boolean `true`.
- Forgetting that a comparison already produces a boolean, and accidentally returning a number or a string from a “yes or no” function.

## In practice

When you name a function `isEmpty`, `hasPermission`, or `isLess`, callers expect a boolean. Give them one, as directly as you can. Save `if` for when each branch has real work to do, not just a different boolean literal.

## Key takeaways

- A comparison such as `a < b` already evaluates to `true` or `false`.
- You can return that comparison directly instead of wrapping it in `if`.
- Early returns let you handle special cases first and keep the main path un-nested.

A boolean-returning function is often used as the condition of another `if`, or as a filter. Keeping the body to a single comparison makes those call sites easier to read: `if (isLess(a, b))` already says what you mean, without a nested decision inside the helper.

## The card-counting rule

This lesson builds a small card-counting helper. The idea is a running count that changes with each card, and a recommendation to “Bet” or “Hold” based on that count.

The rule Beau uses is:

- Cards `2`, `3`, `4`, `5`, and `6` raise the count by `1`.
- Cards `7`, `8`, and `9` leave the count unchanged.
- Cards `10`, `"J"`, `"Q"`, `"K"`, and `"A"` lower the count by `1`.

After updating the count, if the count is positive the function returns the count plus `" Bet"`. Otherwise it returns the count plus `" Hold"`.

The count has to survive between calls, so it is stored in a variable outside the function (a global in this exercise). Each new card changes that same number.

## Implementing it

A `switch` (or a set of `if` tests) is a natural way to update the count, especially if you group the cards that share a change. Then you build the return string from the current count and the decision:

```js
var count = 0;

function cc(card) {
  switch (card) {
    case 2:
    case 3:
    case 4:
    case 5:
    case 6:
      count += 1;
      break;
    case 10:
    case "J":
    case "Q":
    case "K":
    case "A":
      count -= 1;
      break;
    // 7, 8, 9: no change
  }

  var decision = count > 0 ? "Bet" : "Hold";
  return count + " " + decision;
}

console.log(cc(2));
console.log(cc(3));
console.log(cc(7));
console.log(cc("K"));
console.log(cc("A"));
// Expected output:
// 1 Bet
// 2 Bet
// 2 Bet
// 1 Bet
// 0 Hold
```

(The exact running values depend on the sequence of cards. In this example the count goes 1, 2, 2, 1, 0.)

Notice that face cards and aces are strings, while low cards may be numbers. The `switch` uses strict matching, so `10` the number and `"10"` the string would be different cases if both appeared. Stick to the types used in the lesson.

The space in `" Bet"` or the explicit `" "` when you concatenate is easy to miss and is part of the expected return format: a number, a space, and a word.

## Common pitfalls

- Resetting `count` to `0` inside the function, which throws away the running total.
- Using `>= 0` for the bet decision, so a count of `0` says “Bet” instead of “Hold”. The lesson bets only when the count is positive.
- Forgetting to handle `10` and the face cards as “minus one.”
- Dropping the space in the returned string.

## In practice

This is a pattern you will reuse: keep some state outside a function, update it on each event, and return both the new state and a decision. Whether the “event” is a card, a click, or a network message, the structure is the same.

## Key takeaways

- Low cards (2–6) add one to the count; 10s and face cards subtract one; 7–9 do nothing.
- The count is stored outside the function so it can accumulate.
- A positive count means “Bet”; zero or negative means “Hold”.
- The return value is the count, a space, and the decision word.

## Objects store named values

An object is a collection of properties: names (also called keys) paired with values. Where an array is ordered and indexed by number, an object is labeled by a name. That makes objects a natural way to describe one thing with several attributes, such as a dog with a name, legs, and a tail.

You write an object literal in curly braces, with each property written as `name: value` and commas between them:

```js
var ourDog = {
  name: "Camper",
  legs: 4,
  tails: 1,
  friends: ["everything!"]
};
```

Property names can be written without quotes when they are valid identifiers. If a name has a space in it, you must quote it, and you will need bracket notation to read it.

## Reading and updating properties

Dot notation is the usual way to read or change a property when you know the name in advance:

```js
var myDog = { name: "Coder", legs: 4, tails: 1, friends: ["freeCodeCamp Campers"] };
console.log(myDog.name);
myDog.name = "Happy Coder";
console.log(myDog.name);
// Expected output:
// Coder
// Happy Coder
```

You can also add a brand-new property by assigning to a name that is not there yet, and you can remove a property with `delete`:

```js
myDog.bark = "woof";
delete myDog.tails;
console.log(myDog.bark);
console.log(myDog.tails);
// Expected output:
// woof
// undefined
```

After `delete myDog.tails`, that property is gone. Reading it yields `undefined`.

Bracket notation is the other way to reach a property. It is required when the name is in a variable, or when the name is not a valid identifier (for example `"an end"`):

```js
var testObj = { "an entree": "hamburger", "my side": "veggies", "the drink": "water" };
var value = testObj["an entree"];
var drink = "the drink";
console.log(testObj[drink]);
// Expected output:
// water
```

`testObj.drink` would look for a property literally called `drink`. `testObj[drink]` uses the string stored in the variable `drink`, which is `"the drink"`.

## Common pitfalls

- Using a hyphen or space in a property name and then trying to read it with a dot.
- Writing `obj[drink]` without quotes when you meant the literal name, or with quotes when you meant a variable.
- Assuming `delete` sets a property to `null` rather than removing it.

## In practice

Whenever you would otherwise invent a handful of related variables (`dogName`, `dogLegs`, `dogTails`), put them on one object instead. You can pass that object around as a single value, update one field, and leave the others alone.

## Key takeaways

- Objects store named properties in `{ key: value }` form.
- Dot notation reads and updates a known identifier (`obj.name`).
- Bracket notation handles names in variables and names that need quotes (`obj[name]`).
- You can add a property by assigning to a new name, and remove one with `delete`.

## Using an object as a lookup table

A long `switch` or `else if` chain that only maps a value to another value can often be replaced by an object. You put the answers in the object once, then use bracket notation to pick the property whose name matches the input.

```js
function phoneticLookup(val) {
  var lookup = {
    alpha: "Adams",
    bravo: "Boston",
    charlie: "Chicago",
    delta: "Denver",
    echo: "Easy",
    foxtrot: "Franklin"
  };
  return lookup[val];
}

console.log(phoneticLookup("charlie"));
console.log(phoneticLookup("foxtrot"));
// Expected output:
// Chicago
// Franklin
```

`lookup[val]` is the whole point. If `val` is `"charlie"`, the function returns `"Chicago"`. If the name is not in the object, the result is `undefined`.

This is easier to extend than a switch: add another property, and a new input starts working. It is also a good use of bracket notation, because the property name is not known until the function runs.

## Checking whether a property exists

Reading a missing property gives `undefined`, but so does a property that exists and is set to `undefined`. To ask “does this object have this key?”, use `hasOwnProperty`:

```js
var myObj = { gift: "pony", pet: "kitten", bed: "sleigh" };

function checkObj(obj, checkProp) {
  if (obj.hasOwnProperty(checkProp)) {
    return obj[checkProp];
  }
  return "Not Found";
}

console.log(checkObj(myObj, "gift"));
console.log(checkObj(myObj, "house"));
// Expected output:
// pony
// Not Found
```

`hasOwnProperty` returns a boolean. Combine it with bracket notation to return either the stored value or a clear fallback.

## Common pitfalls

- Using dot notation when the name is in a variable (`lookup.val` looks up the literal key `"val"`).
- Treating a missing key as the string `"undefined"` instead of the value `undefined`.
- Forgetting `hasOwnProperty` and assuming every `undefined` result means “not in the object.”

## In practice

Lookup objects are a clean way to store dictionaries: country codes, status labels, configuration flags. When you catch yourself writing twelve almost identical `case` branches, consider an object instead.

## Key takeaways

- An object can replace a switch when you are mapping exact keys to values.
- Use bracket notation with a variable to select the property.
- `hasOwnProperty` tells you whether a key is present.

This is also a stepping stone to later lessons, where you look up a profile or a record by id. Once you are comfortable selecting `obj[key]` and asking `hasOwnProperty`, those later functions are mostly a loop plus this same lookup.

## Objects and arrays inside objects

Property values can themselves be objects or arrays. That lets you model richer data, such as a storage box that contains several collections, or a player with a list of friends and an inventory.

```js
var myStorage = {
  car: {
    inside: { "glove box": "maps", "passenger seat": "crumbs" },
    outside: { trunk: "jack" }
  }
};

var gloveBoxContents = myStorage.car.inside["glove box"];
console.log(gloveBoxContents);
// Expected output:
// maps
```

You walk the path one step at a time: `myStorage.car` is an object, `.inside` is an object, and `["glove box"]` needs brackets because of the space in the name. Mixing dots and brackets in one expression is normal.

## Nested arrays

You can also nest arrays, or mix arrays and objects. Access follows the same rule: use `[]` with a number for an array index, and a dot or brackets for an object property.

```js
var myPlants = [
  { type: "flowers", list: ["rose", "tulip", "dandelion"] },
  { type: "trees", list: ["fir", "pine", "birch"] }
];

var secondTree = myPlants[1].list[1];
console.log(secondTree);
// Expected output:
// pine
```

`myPlants[1]` is the second object (`trees`). `.list` is that object’s array. `[1]` is the second name in the list, `"pine"`.

The data can look busy on the page, but the access path is just a sequence of choices: which item, which field, which index. Draw the path on paper if you get lost, then translate it into code.

## Common pitfalls

- Using the wrong kind of access: a dot on an array index, or a number in a dot (`plants.1`).
- Forgetting quotes around a property name that contains a space.
- Going one level too shallow and logging a whole object or array when you wanted a single string.

## In practice

Real records are almost always nested: a user with an address object, a playlist with an array of tracks, a product with a list of variants. Get comfortable chaining access, and you can reach any field without flattening the data first.

## Key takeaways

- Object properties can hold other objects or arrays.
- Mix dot notation, bracket notation, and numeric indexes to walk a path.
- Quoted keys with spaces must be read with brackets.

If a path looks long, assign an intermediate value (`var inside = myStorage.car.inside`) and then read the next field from that name. The result is the same, and the line is easier to check when you are debugging a typo in the path.

## A collection of records

This lesson is a small database problem. You have an object whose keys are album ids, and each album is an object that may have a `artist`, a `album` title, and a `tracks` array. Your job is to write `updateRecords(id, prop, value)` that updates the collection according to a set of rules.

A slightly simplified picture of the data looks like this:

```js
var collection = {
  2548: {
    album: "Slippery When Wet",
    artist: "Bon Jovi",
    tracks: ["Let It Rock", "You Give Love a Bad Name"]
  },
  2468: {
    album: "1999",
    artist: "Prince",
    tracks: ["1999", "Little Red Corvette"]
  }
};
```

The function has to handle several cases, which is why it is a good exercise in conditionals plus nested objects.

## The update rules

The rules Beau works from are:

- If `value` is an empty string, delete the property `prop` from that album.
- If `prop` is not `"tracks"` and `value` is not empty, set that property to `value`.
- If `prop` is `"tracks"` and the album does not already have a `tracks` array, create an empty array first.
- If `prop` is `"tracks"` and `value` is not empty, append `value` to the `tracks` array.

Then return the whole collection.

```js
function updateRecords(id, prop, value) {
  if (value === "") {
    delete collection[id][prop];
  } else if (prop !== "tracks") {
    collection[id][prop] = value;
  } else {
    if (!collection[id].hasOwnProperty("tracks")) {
      collection[id].tracks = [];
    }
    collection[id].tracks.push(value);
  }
  return collection;
}
```

Walk one example. `updateRecords(2468, "tracks", "Free")` should keep Prince’s existing tracks and add `"Free"` at the end. `updateRecords(2548, "artist", "")` should delete the artist from album 2548. `updateRecords(2548, "album", "Something New")` should change the title.

The empty-string rule is easy to miss: it is how the function deletes, not how it stores a blank title.

Ids are object keys, so `collection[id]` needs bracket notation because `id` is a parameter, not a literal property name.

## Common pitfalls

- Assigning a track string directly to `tracks` and wiping the array, instead of pushing onto it.
- Forgetting to create `tracks` when it does not exist yet, which makes `.push` fail.
- Treating `""` as a normal value instead of a delete signal.
- Updating a copy and forgetting to return `collection`.

## In practice

This is a miniature version of a real update API: look up a record by id, then add, change, or delete a field according to rules. Breaking the rules into an ordered set of `if` tests keeps the function readable.

## Key takeaways

- The collection is an object of objects, keyed by album id.
- An empty `value` means “delete this property.”
- Non-track properties are set directly; tracks are stored in an array and new names are appended.
- Create a `tracks` array if the album does not already have one.

## Repeating work with loops

A loop runs a block of code more than once. Beau covers the three classic loops in JavaScript: `while`, `for`, and `do...while`, then uses them to walk and build arrays, including nested loops.

## while

A `while` loop repeats as long as its condition is true. You are responsible for changing something inside the body so the condition can eventually become false. If you do not, you get an infinite loop.

```js
var myArray = [];
var i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
  myArray.push(i);
  i++;
}
console.log(myArray);
// Expected output:
// [ 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 ]
```

## for

A `for` loop packs the common pattern “start, check, update” into one line: initialization, condition, and increment.

```js
var ourArray = [];
for (var i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  ourArray.push(i);
}
console.log(ourArray);
// Expected output:
// [ 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 ]
```

You can count the other way, or skip by twos, by changing the increment and the condition. For even numbers up to ten you might write `for (var i = 0; i < 10; i += 2)`. For a countdown, `for (var i = 10; i > 0; i--)` works, as long as the condition eventually fails.

A very common use is to walk an existing array with `i = 0; i < arr.length; i++` and read `arr[i]`.

```js
var total = 0;
var arr = [2, 3, 4, 5, 6];
for (var i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
  total += arr[i];
}
console.log(total);
// Expected output:
// 20
```

## do...while

A `do...while` loop runs the body *once* before it checks the condition. Even if the condition is already false, the body still runs that first time.

```js
var myArray = [];
var i = 10;
do {
  myArray.push(i);
  i++;
} while (i < 5);
console.log(myArray, i);
// Expected output:
// [ 10 ] 11
```

A plain `while (i < 5)` starting at `i = 10` would not have pushed anything. That difference is the reason this loop exists.

## Nested loops

Loops can sit inside loops. A typical use is walking a nested array: the outer loop chooses the inner array, and the inner loop walks its elements.

```js
function multiplyAll(arr) {
  var product = 1;
  for (var i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    for (var j = 0; j < arr[i].length; j++) {
      product *= arr[i][j];
    }
  }
  return product;
}

console.log(multiplyAll([[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6, 7]]));
// Expected output:
// 5040
```

Use different index names (`i` and `j`) so the inner loop does not reset the outer one.

## Common pitfalls

- Writing a condition that never becomes false, which hangs the program.
- Using `<= arr.length` and walking off the end of the array (`undefined` in the last step).
- Putting a semicolon right after `for (...)` so the following block is not actually the loop body.
- Reusing `i` in a nested loop and breaking the outer count.

## In practice

If you know how many times you will repeat, or you are walking an array by index, start with `for`. If you are waiting for a condition that is not a simple counter, use `while`. Use `do...while` only when the body must run at least once.

## Key takeaways

- `while` checks the condition, then maybe runs the body.
- `for` combines initialization, condition, and increment.
- `do...while` always runs the body once, then checks.
- Nested loops can walk arrays of arrays.
- Something in the body or increment must make the condition false eventually.

## Searching a list of profiles

This lesson walks an array of objects and looks for a matching name, then returns a requested property. It combines loops, object access, and early returns.

A typical contacts list looks like this:

```js
var contacts = [
  { firstName: "Akira", lastName: "Laine", number: "0543236543", likes: ["Pizza", "Coding", "Brownie Points"] },
  { firstName: "Harry", lastName: "Potter", number: "0994372684", likes: ["Hogwarts", "Magic", "Hagrid"] },
  { firstName: "Sherlock", lastName: "Holmes", number: "0487345643", likes: ["Intriguing Cases", "Violin"] },
  { firstName: "Kristian", lastName: "Vos", number: "unknown", likes: ["JavaScript", "Gaming", "Foxes"] }
];
```

The function `lookUpProfile(name, prop)` should:

- Find the contact whose `firstName` is `name`.
- If that contact has a property `prop`, return the value of that property.
- If the name is found but `prop` is not, return `"No such property"`.
- If no contact has that first name, return `"No such contact"`.

## A straightforward implementation

Loop through the array. When you find a matching first name, check the property and return immediately. If the loop finishes without a match, the name was not in the list.

```js
function lookUpProfile(name, prop) {
  for (var i = 0; i < contacts.length; i++) {
    if (contacts[i].firstName === name) {
      if (contacts[i].hasOwnProperty(prop)) {
        return contacts[i][prop];
      }
      return "No such property";
    }
  }
  return "No such contact";
}

console.log(lookUpProfile("Akira", "likes"));
console.log(lookUpProfile("Harry", "address"));
console.log(lookUpProfile("Bob", "number"));
// Expected output:
// [ 'Pizza', 'Coding', 'Brownie Points' ]
// No such property
// No such contact
```

The inner check uses `hasOwnProperty` so a missing field is not confused with a field whose value happens to be falsy. Bracket notation is required for `prop` because the property name arrives as a parameter.

The `"No such contact"` return sits *after* the loop. If you put it inside the loop, the first non-matching contact would already report that the name does not exist, even if a later contact would have matched.

## Common pitfalls

- Returning `"No such contact"` inside the loop, on the first mismatch.
- Using dot notation for `prop`.
- Checking the property before you have confirmed the name, or the other way around, and returning the wrong error string.
- Forgetting to return the property value when both name and property exist.

## In practice

This is a standard “search a list of records” function. You will write variations of it for users, products, and messages: loop, match an id or name, then either return a field or a clear error.

## Key takeaways

- Walk the array of profiles with a `for` loop.
- When the name matches, return the property or `"No such property"`.
- Return `"No such contact"` only after every profile has been checked.
- Use `hasOwnProperty` and bracket notation for the requested field.

## Random numbers

`Math.random()` returns a number in the range `[0, 1)`: at least 0, and always less than 1. It is the starting point for almost every random value you will generate in beginner JavaScript.

To get a random whole number, scale the result and then remove the decimal part. `Math.floor` rounds down, which pairs well with `Math.random()`:

```js
var r = Math.floor(Math.random() * 10);
console.log(r);
// Expected output: a whole number from 0 to 9 inclusive
```

`Math.random() * 10` is in `[0, 10)`. Flooring it yields `0` through `9`. If you want `0` through `n` inclusive, multiply by `n + 1`.

To pick a whole number in an arbitrary range from `myMin` to `myMax`, inclusive, use this pattern:

```js
function randomRange(myMin, myMax) {
  return Math.floor(Math.random() * (myMax - myMin + 1)) + myMin;
}

console.log(randomRange(5, 10));
// Expected output: a whole number from 5 to 10 inclusive
```

`myMax - myMin + 1` is how many integers sit in that range. You scale the random fraction to that many possibilities, floor it, then shift the result up by `myMin`.

## parseInt

`parseInt` reads a string and returns an integer. It stops at the first character that cannot be part of the number. If it cannot read a number at the start of the string, it returns `NaN`.

```js
console.log(parseInt("007"));
console.log(parseInt("12px"));
console.log(parseInt("abc"));
// Expected output:
// 7
// 12
// NaN
```

`parseInt` can also take a second argument, the *radix* (base). `parseInt("11", 2)` interprets `"11"` as binary and returns `3`. `parseInt("11", 10)` is ordinary decimal 11. Passing the radix is a good habit, because older engines sometimes guessed the base from a leading `0`.

```js
console.log(parseInt("11", 2));
console.log(parseInt("11", 10));
// Expected output:
// 3
// 11
```

## Common pitfalls

- Forgetting `+ 1` in a range, so the maximum value is never produced.
- Using `Math.round` instead of `Math.floor`, which changes the odds at the ends of the range.
- Calling `parseInt` without a radix, or assuming `parseInt("3.9")` will return `4` (it returns `3`, because it reads an integer, it does not round).

## In practice

Random integers show up in games, quizzes, and shuffled lists. `parseInt` shows up whenever user input or text from a page needs to be treated as a number. Together they are small tools, but you will reach for them often.

## Key takeaways

- `Math.random()` is in `[0, 1)`.
- `Math.floor(Math.random() * n)` gives `0` through `n - 1`.
- A inclusive range from min to max uses `Math.floor(Math.random() * (max - min + 1)) + min`.
- `parseInt(string, radix)` converts text to an integer in the given base.

## A compact if / else

The conditional (ternary) operator is a one-line version of `if` / `else` that produces a value. Its shape is `condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse`.

```js
function findGreater(a, b) {
  return a > b ? "a is greater" : "b is greater or equal";
}

console.log(findGreater(5, 3));
console.log(findGreater(3, 5));
// Expected output:
// a is greater
// b is greater or equal
```

That is the same logic as an `if` that returns one string and an `else` that returns the other, written as a single expression. Because it is an expression, you can return it directly or assign it to a variable.

## Nesting ternaries

You can nest ternary operators to express more than two branches, the way you would chain `else if`. Read them from the first condition downward:

```js
function findGreaterOrEqual(a, b) {
  return a === b
    ? "a and b are equal"
    : a > b
      ? "a is greater"
      : "b is greater";
}

console.log(findGreaterOrEqual(3, 3));
console.log(findGreaterOrEqual(5, 2));
console.log(findGreaterOrEqual(1, 4));
// Expected output:
// a and b are equal
// a is greater
// b is greater
```

This works, and you will see it in the course, but once there are several branches an `if` / `else if` chain is often easier to scan. Use a nested ternary when the branches are short values, not large blocks of work.

A ternary always needs both the `?` side and the `:` side. It is not a standalone `if`. If you only want to run a side effect when a condition is true, a plain `if` is the right tool.

## Common pitfalls

- Leaving out the `: ...` part, which is a syntax error.
- Nesting so many ternaries that the line is hard to parenthesize correctly.
- Using a ternary for its side effects instead of its value.

## In practice

Ternaries shine when you are picking between two short values: a label, a CSS class, a default, a fallback string. When a branch needs several statements, switch back to `if`.

## Key takeaways

- The ternary operator is `condition ? ifTrue : ifFalse`.
- It produces a value, so you can return it or assign it.
- Nested ternaries can stand in for `else if`, but they should stay readable.

Read a ternary as a sentence: “if this is true, use that value, otherwise use this other value.” If you cannot say the sentence out loud without getting lost, the expression is too nested and an `if` block will serve you better.

## Why let and const exist

The earlier lessons declared variables with `var`. ES6 adds `let` and `const`, which fix several of `var`’s quirks.

`var` is function-scoped: a `var` inside an `if` or a `for` is still visible throughout the function. You can also declare the same `var` name twice in one scope, and you can use a `var` before its declaration (it will be `undefined` because of hoisting). Those behaviors cause subtle bugs.

`let` is *block-scoped*. A block is anything in curly braces: a loop, an `if`, or a standalone `{ ... }`. A `let` declared inside a block is not visible outside it. You also cannot redeclare the same `let` name in the same scope.

```js
var catName = "Quincy";
var quote;
var catName = "Beau"; // allowed with var, easy to do by accident

let dogName = "Snoopy";
// let dogName = "Odie"; // SyntaxError in the same scope
dogName = "Odie"; // this assignment is fine
```

If you wrap `let` in a block, the inner name can exist next to an outer one without overwriting it:

```js
let numArray = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  numArray.push(i);
}
// console.log(i); // i is not visible here when it was declared with let
console.log(numArray);
// Expected output:
// [ 0, 1, 2 ]
```

With `var i`, that `i` would still be visible (and equal to `3`) after the loop. With `let`, the loop counter stays inside the loop, which is usually what you wanted.

## const

`const` is also block-scoped, but you must assign it when you declare it, and you cannot reassign the name later.

```js
const PI = 3.14;
// PI = 3; // TypeError
```

“Cannot reassign” is not the same as “immutable.” If the value is an object or an array, the name still points at the same object, but the contents of that object can change:

```js
const s = [5, 6, 7];
s[2] = 45; // allowed
// s = [1, 2, 3]; // not allowed
console.log(s);
// Expected output:
// [ 5, 6, 45 ]
```

If you want an object or array that cannot be changed at all, you can pass it to `Object.freeze`. After freezing, assigning to an element or property fails (strictly, it throws in strict mode and is ignored otherwise).

```js
const MATH = Object.freeze({ PI: 3.14 });
// MATH.PI = 99; // does not change MATH.PI when frozen
console.log(MATH.PI);
// Expected output:
// 3.14
```

A common style is: use `const` by default, and switch to `let` when you know the name must be reassigned. Avoid `var` in new code.

## Common pitfalls

- Redeclaring a `let` or `const` in the same scope.
- Expecting `const` to freeze an array or object; it only locks the binding.
- Using a `let` or `const` before its line, which is not allowed the way a hoisted `var` is.
- Declaring a `const` without an initializer.

## In practice

When you write new variables in this course’s later exercises, prefer `const` and `let`. You will get earlier errors for typos (redeclaring a name, using one outside its block) instead of silent global leaks.

## Key takeaways

- `let` and `const` are block-scoped; `var` is function-scoped.
- `let` can be reassigned but not redeclared in the same scope.
- `const` cannot be reassigned; objects and arrays it points to can still be mutated unless you freeze them.
- Prefer `const` unless you need to reassign, then use `let`.

## Arrow functions

ES6 introduces a shorter way to write a function, especially a small function you pass to another function or return from one.

```js
const myFunc = function() {
  const myVar = "value";
  return myVar;
};

const myFuncArrow = () => {
  const myVar = "value";
  return myVar;
};
```

If the body is a single expression, you can drop the braces and the `return`. The expression’s value is returned automatically:

```js
const myFuncShort = () => "value";
console.log(myFuncShort());
// Expected output:
// value
```

A single parameter can also drop its parentheses: `const doubler = item => item * 2`. With zero or two or more parameters, the parentheses stay.

Arrow functions are often used as callbacks, for example with array methods, because they keep a short operation on one line.

## Default parameters

You can give a parameter a default value that is used when the caller omits the argument (or passes `undefined`):

```js
const increment = (number, value = 1) => number + value;
console.log(increment(5, 2));
console.log(increment(5));
// Expected output:
// 7
// 6
```

If the second argument is missing, `value` is `1`. This replaces a common older pattern of writing `value = value || 1` inside the body, which had the flaw of treating a genuine `0` as missing.

## Rest parameters

A rest parameter gathers leftover arguments into a real array. You write it as `...name` in the last position of the parameter list.

```js
function howMany(...args) {
  return "You have passed " + args.length + " arguments.";
}

const sum = (...args) => {
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
    total += args[i];
  }
  return total;
};

console.log(sum(0, 1, 2));
console.log(sum(5));
// Expected output:
// 3
// 5
```

Unlike the old `arguments` object, a rest parameter is a real array, so you can use array methods on it.

## Spread

The other use of `...` is spread: it expands an array (or another iterable) into individual elements. That is useful when you want to copy an array, or pass its items as arguments, or combine arrays.

```js
const arr1 = [1, 2, 3];
const arr2 = [...arr1, 4, 5];
console.log(arr2);
Math.max(...[4, 1, 9]);
// Expected output of arr2:
// [ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 ]
```

`Math.max(...[4, 1, 9])` is the same as `Math.max(4, 1, 9)`. Spread in an array literal is also a clear way to copy: `const copy = [...original]`.

## Common pitfalls

- Adding braces to a one-line arrow function and forgetting `return`.
- Putting rest anywhere other than the last parameter.
- Confusing rest (collect arguments into an array) with spread (expand an array into elements).
- Giving a default of `value = value || 1` and accidentally replacing a valid `0`.

## In practice

These tools make function signatures more honest: defaults document optional inputs, rest documents “any number of arguments,” and arrows keep small callbacks short. Spread makes copies and combinations without mutating the original array.

## Key takeaways

- `const fn = (a, b) => a + b` is a compact function that returns an expression.
- Default parameters fill in a value when an argument is omitted.
- Rest (`...args`) gathers leftover arguments into an array.
- Spread (`...array`) expands an array into elements or into another literal.

## Destructuring assignments

Destructuring is a concise way to unpack values from objects and arrays into variables.

From an object, you can copy properties into names that match the keys:

```js
const user = { name: "John Doe", age: 34 };
const { name, age } = user;
console.log(name, age);
// Expected output:
// John Doe 34
```

That is the same as `const name = user.name` and `const age = user.age`. You can also rename while you unpack: `const { name: userName, age: userAge } = user` creates `userName` and `userAge`.

You can destructure nested objects by nesting the pattern, and you can destructure in a function’s parameter list so the function receives an object and names its fields immediately.

From an array, the pattern is positional:

```js
const [a, b] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
console.log(a, b);
// Expected output:
// 1 2
```

You can skip items with extra commas, and you can collect the rest of the array with `...`:

```js
const [first, , third, ...rest] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
console.log(first, third, rest);
// Expected output:
// 1 3 [ 4, 5 ]
```

A useful trick is swapping two variables without a temporary name:

```js
let x = 1;
let y = 2;
[x, y] = [y, x];
console.log(x, y);
// Expected output:
// 2 1
```

You can also destructure the return value of a function if that function returns an array or an object.

## Template literals

Template literals are strings written with backticks (`` ` ``). They can span multiple lines, and they can embed expressions in `${...}`:

```js
const person = { name: "Zodiac Hasbro", age: 56 };
const greeting = `Hello, my name is ${person.name}!
I am ${person.age} years old.`;
console.log(greeting);
// Expected output:
// Hello, my name is Zodiac Hasbro!
// I am 56 years old.
```

The `${}` placeholder can hold any expression, not just a variable: a calculation, a function call, or a property access. You no longer have to concatenate with `+` and remember the spaces around every piece.

Together, destructuring and templates are a cleaner way to take data apart and to put messages back together.

## Common pitfalls

- Using quotes instead of backticks for a template literal, so `${name}` is taken literally.
- Destructuring a key that is not on the object and not noticing you have `undefined`.
- Forgetting that array destructuring is by position, not by name.
- Trying to use `...rest` anywhere other than the end of the pattern.

## In practice

When a function needs two or three fields from a larger object, destructure them in the parameter list. When you build a string that mixes words and values, prefer a template literal over a chain of `+`.

## Key takeaways

- Object destructuring unpacks properties into variables, optionally renaming them.
- Array destructuring unpacks by position and can skip items or collect the rest.
- You can swap variables with `[a, b] = [b, a]`.
- Template literals use backticks and `${expression}` to build strings, including multi-line text.

These two features often travel together in real code. You unpack a record with destructuring, then immediately format a message with a template literal: take `name` and `age` off a person object, then write a greeting that includes both values. That workflow replaces several lines of property access and string concatenation, and it is worth practicing until it feels natural. When a pattern grows too nested, assign an inner object to an intermediate name first, then destructure that — clarity beats squeezing everything into one line.

One more way to think about it: destructuring is for taking a structure apart, and a template literal is for putting a string together. You will often do those two jobs in that order in the same function. Get the fields you need, with clear names, then write the sentence or report you want to show. If a destructuring pattern starts to look like a pile of nested braces, unpack one level at a time. If a template starts to hide logic inside the placeholder, compute that value on the line above and then embed the already-named result. Small steps stay readable, and readable notes are easier to trust when you come back later.

## Shorthand in object literals

ES6 makes object literals less repetitive when a property name already matches a variable name, and when you want to write a method.

```js
const getMousePosition = (x, y) => ({ x, y });
// same as { x: x, y: y }
```

If the variable is `x`, `{ x }` creates a property `x` whose value is that variable. This is called a shorthand property.

Methods have a shorthand too. Instead of `name: function() { ... }`, you can write `name() { ... }` inside the literal:

```js
const bicycle = {
  gear: 2,
  setGear(newGear) {
    this.gear = newGear;
  }
};
bicycle.setGear(3);
console.log(bicycle.gear);
// Expected output:
// 3
```

`this` inside the method refers to the object the method was called on, so `this.gear` is `bicycle.gear` in that call.

## class syntax

ES6 also adds `class` as a clearer way to write constructor functions and their methods. A class has a `constructor` that runs when you call `new`, and other methods that go on the instance.

```js
class Vegetable {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }
}

const carrot = new Vegetable("carrot");
console.log(carrot.name);
// Expected output:
// carrot
```

Getters and setters let you run code when a property is read or assigned, while still using property syntax at the call site:

```js
class Thermostat {
  constructor(fahrenheit) {
    this._fahrenheit = fahrenheit;
  }
  get temperature() {
    return (5 / 9) * (this._fahrenheit - 32);
  }
  set temperature(celsius) {
    this._fahrenheit = (celsius * 9.0) / 5 + 32;
  }
}

const thermos = new Thermostat(76);
console.log(thermos.temperature);
thermos.temperature = 26;
console.log(thermos.temperature);
// Expected output (approximately):
// 24.44
// 26
```

In this example the constructor takes Fahrenheit, the getter returns Celsius, and the setter accepts Celsius and stores Fahrenheit. The caller writes `thermos.temperature` without calling it as a function. The leading underscore on `_fahrenheit` is a convention meaning “internal field,” not a language rule.

You will also see simple classes with a constructor and a couple of methods, without getters. The habit to learn is: initialize instance data in `constructor`, and put behavior in named methods.

## Common pitfalls

- Forgetting `new` when constructing a class instance.
- Writing a getter as `temperature()` at the call site; it is accessed as a property.
- Mutating `this` in a shorthand object method and losing `this` if you pull the function off the object and call it alone (a later topic, but a reason to be careful with callbacks).
- Assuming `{ x }` works when the variable has a different name; in that case you still write `{ x: otherName }`.

## In practice

Use shorthand properties to return a small object from a function without repeating names. Use a class when several instances should share the same methods and each should carry its own data, such as a thermostat, a rectangle, or a player.

## Key takeaways

- `{ name }` is shorthand for `{ name: name }`.
- Object methods can be written as `method() { ... }`.
- `class` groups a constructor and methods; create instances with `new`.
- Getters and setters let you intercept property reads and writes.

## Sharing code between files

In older JavaScript, especially in Node.js, people often used `require` to load code from another file. ES6 modules use `export` and `import` instead. You mark the values a file wants to share, then import them by name in another file.

## Named exports

A named export attaches a name to a function, class, or constant. You can export it on the declaration, or list names in an `export { ... }` statement.

```js
// math.js
export const add = (x, y) => x + y;
export const subtract = (x, y) => x - y;
```

In another file you import those names with curly braces. The names have to match, unless you rename them with `as`.

```js
import { add, subtract } from "./math.js";
console.log(add(2, 3));
// Expected output:
// 5
```

You can also import everything that the file exports as one object: `import * as math from "./math.js"`, then call `math.add(2, 3)`.

## Default exports

A file can also have one default export. That is useful when the file is mainly about a single function or class.

```js
// shout.js
export default function shout(text) {
  return text.toUpperCase();
}
```

A default import does not use curly braces, and you can give it any local name:

```js
import shout from "./shout.js";
console.log(shout("hello"));
// Expected output:
// HELLO
```

You can mix a default export with named exports in the same file, and import them together: `import shout, { add } from "./file.js"`.

The older `require` function still exists in some environments, but this lesson’s point is the newer syntax: `export` what a module provides, `import` what another module needs, using relative paths such as `"./math.js"`.

## Common pitfalls

- Importing a named export without curly braces, or a default export with them.
- Misspelling the named export; the name in `import { add }` must match the exported name.
- Forgetting the file path or the `.js` extension in environments that require it.
- Expecting `require` and `import` to be interchangeable in every environment without configuration.

## In practice

Once a file grows, split reusable helpers into a module and import them. Named exports are a good default when a file provides several functions. A default export is a good default when a file provides one main thing.

## Key takeaways

- ES6 modules use `export` and `import` instead of the older `require`.
- Named exports are imported with curly braces and matching names.
- A file may have one default export, imported without braces.
- `import * as name` gathers all named exports into one object.
